---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageIntro from '../../../components/PageIntro.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="0014. Author normative documents as Markdown in the website repository | Macro Evidence"
  description="Governance decision 0014: Author normative documents as Markdown in the website repository. Status: Accepted."
>
  <PageIntro
    kicker="Governance decision"
    title="0014. Author normative documents as Markdown in the website repository"
    lead="Governance decision 0014 of the Macro Evidence Governance decision series."
  />

  <section class="section policy-page">
    <div class="site-container reading policy-reading-wide">
      <article class="policy-text">
        <p class="policy-status">
          <strong>Status:</strong>&#32;Accepted<br />&#32;<strong>Date:</strong
          >&#32;2026-11-28
        </p>
        <h2 id="context">Context</h2>
        <p>
          Governance decision 0013 made the website repository the canonical
          source of Macro Evidence's public governance content. It states that
          each document is written directly as an Astro page. It considered and
          rejected keeping Markdown as the source and rendering it in the
          website. Its reasons: that keeps two authoring formats for one
          published page, and it adds a rendering layer whose only purpose is to
          preserve the old format.
        </p>
        <p>
          The pages published under that decision show what hand-written HTML
          costs for long text. The page sources hold many character entities
          that exist only to keep a space next to an inline element. Code
          samples need escaped angle brackets. The source check reads the status
          line of a policy page with a regular expression over HTML. A
          built-output check rejects words that a formatter has fused to an
          inline tag. A person who owns the wording of a document cannot change
          a sentence without handling tags.
        </p>
        <p>Three alternatives were considered:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Keep hand-written Astro pages. This keeps the costs above and keeps
            checks that must parse HTML.
          </li>
          <li>
            Write the documents in MDX. MDX allows imports, exports and JSX in
            the text, and it reads a brace in the text as an expression.
            Normative text needs none of these, and a brace in legal wording
            must not stop a build. The repository's Markdown lint does not cover
            MDX files.
          </li>
          <li>
            Write the documents as plain Markdown with validated frontmatter in
            a content collection of the website repository, and let Astro build
            the pages from them in the same build as the rest of the site. This
            keeps one authoring format and one source for each page, and adds no
            layer whose purpose is to preserve another format.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <h2 id="decision">Decision</h2>
        <p>
          Write each normative document of the website repository as one plain
          Markdown file with validated frontmatter, in a content collection of
          the website repository. Astro builds the published page from that
          file. Interface pages stay Astro pages.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What this does not change.</strong> One Markdown file for each document
          inside the website repository is not a separate Markdown corpus, content
          repository, mirror or synchronization mechanism in the sense of decision
          0013. There is one repository, one source for each page and one build. The
          address of each document is unchanged.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Rules.</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            A normative document contains no raw HTML, JSX, imports or exports.
            The website build must reject them.
          </li>
          <li>
            The frontmatter of a document records its title, version, status and
            last-updated date. The build must fail when a field is missing,
            unknown or invalid.
          </li>
          <li>
            A note block, the GitHub <code>NOTE</code> alert defined in the Documentation
            Standards, is shown on the published page as a note.
          </li>
          <li>
            MDX may be used for a single document only when a need is shown with
            evidence and the maintainer approves. This decision does not allow
            it by default.
          </li>
          <li>
            Converting an existing page to Markdown changes its format only. Its
            address, text, heading identifiers, link targets and dates do not
            change. A conversion is checked against the built page before it
            merges.
          </li>
          <li>
            A document that exists as an Astro page when this decision is
            accepted stays one until it is converted. Each document has one form
            at any time. A document published after this decision is written as
            Markdown.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>Relation to decision 0013.</strong> This decision supersedes decision
          0013 in part:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            the sentence in the section Decision that begins &quot;Each document
            has one canonical page, written directly as an Astro page&quot;; and
          </li>
          <li>
            the alternative in the section Context that begins &quot;Keep
            Markdown as the source and render it in the website&quot;.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The rest of decision 0013 stays in force. The sentence &quot;There is
          no separate Markdown corpus, content repository, mirror or
          synchronization mechanism&quot; stays and applies as stated above. The
          sentence &quot;The chosen option has no mirror, no second source and
          no rendering layer&quot; stays with the purpose it states: no layer
          exists to preserve a second authoring format.
        </p>
        <h2 id="consequences">Consequences</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            An owner of a document can change its text without handling HTML. A
            change is reviewed as Markdown.
          </li>
          <li>The repository's Markdown lint covers the document files.</li>
          <li>
            The checks that read page markup must read frontmatter and the built
            page instead.
          </li>
          <li>
            The Markdown processor and its plugins become dependencies. They are
            pinned. A change of processor needs a check that the text of every
            converted page is unchanged.
          </li>
          <li>
            Existing pages convert in later changes. Until then, documents exist
            in both forms.
          </li>
          <li>
            A route that the verification scripts do not know fails the release
            verification, as decision 0013 requires.
          </li>
          <li>
            The contribution boundary of decision 0013 applies to the Markdown
            files as it applied to the pages.
          </li>
          <li>
            A material change to this authoring model needs a new decision.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <div class="reference-links">
        <a class="text-link" href="/governance/decisions">
          All Governance decisions →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
